WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
Marcus now understands pooling.

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.220
His next question is practical: does buying life insurance remove the

3
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.800
risk of death? No.

4
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.660
It transfers specified financial consequences under a contract.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.980
To classify a risk-management action, ask what changes.

6
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.840
Does the person avoid an exposure, keep the financial responsibility,

7
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.960
share it, reduce the chance or size of loss,

8
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.980
or transfer specified costs?

9
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.600
One family can use several methods together.

10
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.040
The name of the action matters less than what that action

11
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.750
actually accomplishes. Consider three decisions.

12
00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.650
Marcus chooses not to take part in recreational rock climbing.

13
00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.730
He avoids that particular exposure, although he has not avoided every

14
00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:56.730
risk in life. He installs a handrail on a slippery stairway.

15
00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.610
That reduces the likelihood of a fall; it does not guarantee

16
00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:05.510
safety. He also decides to pay for small, affordable losses from

17
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.910
his own savings. That is retention.

18
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.930
Retention may be intentional or result from simply having no insurance.

19
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:17.270
Savings finance a loss; they do not prevent it.

20
00:01:17.796 --> 00:01:20.996
Sharing spreads a financial burden among participants.

21
00:01:21.356 --> 00:01:23.736
Pooling illustrates that idea across a group.

22
00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:28.876
Transfer moves a specified financial obligation to another party, often

23
00:01:28.876 --> 00:01:33.736
through insurance. Marcus buys a policy that pays a stated benefit

24
00:01:33.736 --> 00:01:34.956
for a covered death.

25
00:01:36.016 --> 00:01:40.176
His family still experiences the loss of a person, while the

26
00:01:40.176 --> 00:01:42.516
insurer has the contractual payment obligation.

27
00:01:43.416 --> 00:01:48.816
Insurance does not transfer every possible consequence, and exclusions

28
00:01:48.876 --> 00:01:50.636
or limits still matter.

29
00:01:51.496 --> 00:01:55.356
Sharing and transfer can both appear in the same insurance arrangement

30
00:01:55.356 --> 00:02:00.498
at different levels. Insurance works best with many similar exposure

31
00:02:00.498 --> 00:02:05.898
units and losses that are accidental from the insured's perspective, definite

32
00:02:05.898 --> 00:02:09.978
enough to identify, and measurable in financial terms.

33
00:02:10.818 --> 00:02:15.878
The probability and cost must be reasonably estimable, and the

34
00:02:15.878 --> 00:02:21.218
premium economically feasible. Large, correlated catastrophes

35
00:02:21.218 --> 00:02:24.878
can strain a pool, so insurers manage concentration.

36
00:02:25.798 --> 00:02:30.298
These are useful characteristics, not a promise that every risk meeting

37
00:02:30.298 --> 00:02:31.978
a checklist will be accepted.

38
00:02:32.998 --> 00:02:35.898
Pure risk offers loss or no loss.

39
00:02:36.438 --> 00:02:41.098
Speculative risk also offers possible gain, such as buying stock hoping

40
00:02:41.098 --> 00:02:44.476
its price rises. Now follow a different contract.

41
00:02:45.316 --> 00:02:49.956
An insurer accepts Marcus's policy, then arranges for a reinsurer to

42
00:02:49.956 --> 00:02:51.756
take part of its insurance risk.

43
00:02:52.456 --> 00:02:56.956
This is reinsurance. The original insurer is the ceding insurer;

44
00:02:57.656 --> 00:02:59.316
the other company is the reinsurer.

45
00:02:59.916 --> 00:03:04.136
Unless a separate legal arrangement changes the policy obligation, Marcus's

46
00:03:04.136 --> 00:03:06.316
insurer still owes the policy benefit.

47
00:03:07.096 --> 00:03:09.596
He does not normally file part of his claim with the

48
00:03:09.596 --> 00:03:14.096
reinsurer. Reinsurance can help manage large individual exposures,

49
00:03:14.556 --> 00:03:18.436
accumulated losses, and the insurer's capacity to write business.

50
00:03:19.266 --> 00:03:24.506
Imagine a family maintains safe stairs, holds an emergency fund,

51
00:03:25.106 --> 00:03:26.646
and purchases life insurance.

52
00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:29.906
The stairs reduce a hazard.

53
00:03:30.666 --> 00:03:32.726
The fund supports retained costs.

54
00:03:34.226 --> 00:03:37.166
The policy transfers covered financial risk.

55
00:03:38.146 --> 00:03:42.746
If the insurer buys reinsurance, that creates another transfer between

56
00:03:42.746 --> 00:03:47.946
insurers. On a question, locate the parties and the event first.

57
00:03:47.946 --> 00:03:50.546
Then identify the action's effect.

58
00:03:51.486 --> 00:03:56.586
Do not call every sensible precaution avoidance, and do not

59
00:03:56.586 --> 00:04:00.646
assume every transfer erases the underlying danger.

60
00:04:01.506 --> 00:04:04.886
Answer the lesson questions using those distinctions.
